\section{Python environment}{

When the package is loaded it declares its Python requirement with
\code{\link[reticulate:py_require]{reticulate::py_require()}}, and reticulate installs it into a managed
environment the first time Python is used. The requirement defaults to the
GitHub repository and can be changed with the option \code{cointf.python}, set
before loading the package:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{options(cointf.python = "cointf @ git+https://github.com/aabiddanda/cointf@dev")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

To use an existing Python environment instead (for example one where
'cointf' was installed with \verb{pip install .} from a clone), point
reticulate at it before loading the package, e.g.
\code{Sys.setenv(RETICULATE_PYTHON = "/path/to/venv/bin/python")}. The GitHub
requirement is then not declared. Setting \code{options(cointf.python = NULL)}
also skips it, for environments selected another way (e.g.
\code{\link[reticulate:use_virtualenv]{reticulate::use_virtualenv()}}).
}

